\HLAClauseHeading{clause-5}{5. Federation rules}
\vspace{22.10pt}

\HLADetailedRuleHeading{detailed-rule-1}{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleOneTitle}
\vspace{10.78pt}

\HLANormativeRuleStatement{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleOneStatement}
\vspace{11.82pt}

All data to be exchanged in accordance with the HLA shall be documented in a FOM. The FOM is\linebreak
specified using one or more FOM modules and one MOM and Initialization Module (MIM). A FOM shall\linebreak
document the agreement among federates on data to be exchanged using the HLA services during\linebreak
federation execution and the minimal set of conditions of the data exchange (e.g., updates to be sent when\linebreak
changes exceed a certain value). As such, a FOM is an essential element in defining a federation. The HLA\linebreak
does not prescribe which data are included in a FOM (this task is the responsibility of the federation user\par
\endgroup
\clearpage

% Native semantic reconstruction of source physical page 33 (printed page 32).
% Provenance: IEEE Std 1516-2025, Rule 1 conclusion and Rules 2--4.
\HLAStartSemanticPage{33}{32}
\fontsize{10.02pt}{11.566pt}\selectfont
\begingroup
\hbadness=10000\relax
\addfontfeatures{FakeStretch=1.025}
\vspace*{0.19pt}

and developer). FOMs shall be documented in the format prescribed in IEEE Std 1516.2-2025 to support\linebreak
the reuse of a FOM by new users for their purposes.\par
\vspace{29.01pt}

\HLADetailedRuleHeading{detailed-rule-2}{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleTwoTitle}
\vspace{12.79pt}

\HLANormativeRuleStatement{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleTwoStatement}
\vspace{11.81pt}

In the HLA, the responsibility for maintaining the values of HLA object instance attributes shall take place\linebreak
in the joined federate. In an HLA federation, all joined federate-associated instance attributes shall be\linebreak
owned by federates, not by the RTI. However, the RTI may own instance attributes associated with the\linebreak
federation MOM. The RTI may use data about instance attributes and interactions to support RTI services\linebreak
(e.g., Declaration Management), but these data are merely used by the RTI, not changed.\par
\vspace{24.96pt}

\HLADetailedRuleHeading{detailed-rule-3}{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleThreeTitle}
\vspace{12.79pt}

\HLANormativeRuleStatement{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleThreeStatement}
\vspace{11.81pt}

The HLA federate interface specification (see IEEE Std 1516.1-2025) specifies a set of interfaces to\linebreak
services in the RTI to support coordinated exchange of instance attribute values and interactions in\linebreak
accordance with a federation’s FOM. Under the HLA, intercommunication of FOM data among joined\linebreak
federates participating in a given federation execution shall be executed by the exchange of data via the\linebreak
RTI services. Based on the FOM, joined federates shall identify to the RTI what information they will\linebreak
provide and require, along with instance attribute and interaction data corresponding to the changing state\linebreak
of object instances in the joined federate. The RTI shall then provide the coordination, synchronization, and\linebreak
data exchange among the joined federates to permit a coherent execution of the federation.\par
\vspace{11.94pt}

Ensuring that the right data are provided at the right times and that the data are used in a substantively\linebreak
correct way shall be the responsibility of the joined federates. The RTI shall ensure that the data are\linebreak
delivered using joined federates in accordance with their declared requirements (e.g., which data, reliability\linebreak
of transport, event ordering) to provide a common view of shared data across a given federation execution,\linebreak
as specified in the FOM.\par
\vspace{11.94pt}

RTI services shall be used to ensure that the coordination needs of the distributed applications (federations)\linebreak
are met consistently across all participants in a federation and over the life of a federation execution.\par
\vspace{24.96pt}

\HLADetailedRuleHeading{detailed-rule-4}{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleFourTitle}
\vspace{12.79pt}

\HLANormativeRuleStatement{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleFourStatement}
\vspace{11.81pt}

The HLA provides a specification for a standard interface between the federate application and the RTI.\linebreak
Joined federates shall use this standard interface to access RTI services (see IEEE Std 1516.1-2025). The\linebreak
specification shall define how federate applications interact with the infrastructure. However, because the\linebreak
interface and the RTI will be used for a wide variety of applications requiring data exchange of diverse\linebreak
characteristics, the interface specification says nothing about the specific federate data to be exchanged\linebreak
over the interface.\par
\endgroup
\clearpage

% Native semantic reconstruction of source physical page 34 (printed page 33).
% Provenance: IEEE Std 1516-2025, Rule 5 and Federate Rules 6--8.
\HLAStartSemanticPage{34}{33}
\fontsize{10.02pt}{11.566pt}\selectfont
\begingroup
\hbadness=10000\relax
\addfontfeatures{FakeStretch=1.025}
\enlargethispage{28pt}
\vspace*{-0.85pt}

\HLADetailedRuleHeading{detailed-rule-5}{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleFiveTitle}
\vspace{12.79pt}

\HLANormativeRuleStatement{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleFiveStatement}
\vspace{11.81pt}

The HLA allows for different joined federates to own different attributes of the same object instance (e.g., a\linebreak
simulation of an aircraft might own the location of the airborne sensor, whereas a sensor system model\linebreak
might own other instance attributes of the sensor). To ensure data coherency across the federation, at most,\linebreak
one joined federate may own any given instance attribute of an object instance at any given time. Joined\linebreak
federates may request that the ownership of instance attributes be acquired or divested dynamically during\linebreak
federation execution. Thus, ownership can be transferred dynamically during execution from one joined\linebreak
federate to another.\par
\vspace{24.35pt}

\HLAClauseHeading{clause-6}{6. Federate rules}
\vspace{13.08pt}

This clause describes the five rules that apply to HLA federates. The rationale for the inclusion of each rule\linebreak
is located in Annex A.\par
\vspace{24.97pt}

\HLADetailedRuleHeading{detailed-rule-6}{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleSixTitle}
\vspace{12.79pt}

\HLANormativeRuleStatement{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleSixStatement}
\vspace{11.81pt}

The HLA SOM shall include the object classes, class attributes, and interaction classes of the federate that\linebreak
can be made public in a federation. The SOM is specified using one or more SOM modules and one\linebreak
optional MIM. The HLA does not prescribe which data are included in the SOM\HLASemicolon{} this task shall be the\linebreak
responsibility of the federate developer. SOMs shall be documented in the format prescribed in IEEE Std\linebreak
1516.2-2025.\par
\vspace{28.94pt}

\HLADetailedRuleHeading{detailed-rule-7}{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleSevenTitle}
\vspace{12.79pt}

\HLANormativeRuleStatement{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleSevenStatement}
\vspace{11.81pt}

The HLA allows for joined federates to make internal object representations and interactions available for\linebreak
external use as part of federation executions. These capabilities for external interaction shall be documented\linebreak
in the SOM for the federate. If documented in the SOM, these federate capabilities shall include the\linebreak
obligation to export updated values of instance attributes that are calculated internally in the federate and\linebreak
the obligation to be able to exercise interactions represented externally (i.e., by other federates in a\linebreak
federation).\par
\vspace{28.94pt}

\HLADetailedRuleHeading{detailed-rule-8}{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleEightTitle}
\vspace{12.79pt}

\HLANormativeRuleStatement{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleEightStatement}
\vspace{11.81pt}

The HLA allows ownership of instance attributes of an object instance to be transferred dynamically during\linebreak
a federation execution. The instance attributes of a federate that can be either owned or reflected and whose\linebreak
ownership can be dynamically acquired or divested during execution shall be documented in the SOM for\linebreak
that federate.\par
\endgroup
\clearpage

% Native semantic reconstruction of source physical page 35 (printed page 34).
% Provenance: IEEE Std 1516-2025, Federate Rules 9 and 10.
\HLAStartSemanticPage{35}{34}
\fontsize{10.02pt}{11.566pt}\selectfont
\begingroup
\hbadness=10000\relax
\addfontfeatures{FakeStretch=1.035}
\vspace*{-0.85pt}

\HLADetailedRuleHeading{detailed-rule-9}{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleNineTitle}
\vspace{12.77pt}

\HLANormativeRuleStatement{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleNineStatement}
\vspace{11.87pt}

The HLA permits federates to own (i.e., provides the privilege to produce updated values for) instance\linebreak
attributes of object instances represented in the federate and to then make those values available to other\linebreak
federates through the RTI. Different federations may specify different conditions under which instance\linebreak
attributes will be updated [e.g., at some specified rate, or when the amount of change in value exceeds a\linebreak
specified threshold (such as altitude changes of more than 1000 ft)]. The conditions applicable to the update\linebreak
of specific instance attributes owned by a federate shall be documented in the SOM for that federate.\par
\vspace{28.90pt}

\HLADetailedRuleHeading{detailed-rule-10}{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleOneZeroTitle}
\vspace{12.77pt}

\HLANormativeRuleStatement{\SemanticRequirementDetailedRuleOneZeroStatement}
\vspace{11.87pt}

Federation designers will identify their time management approach as part of their implementation design.\linebreak
Federates shall adhere to the time management approach of the federation.\par
\endgroup
\clearpage

